  {
    "title": "Protecting the Fourth Amendment: Establishing Guardrails for Automated Surveillance",
    "url": "https://repwalsh.com/News/38819/Press-Releases/Protecting-the-Fourth-Amendment--Establishing-Guardrails-for-Automated-Surveillance",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "Protecting the Fourth Amendment: Establishing Guardrails for Automated Surveillance\nSeptember 25, 2026\nWHAT:\nHouse Republican Policy Committee Chairman Rep. David H. Rowe (R-Snyder/Union/Mifflin/Juniata); Rep. Josh Bashline (R-Clarion/Armstrong); Rep. Brenda Pugh (R-Luzerne); Rep. Jamie Walsh (R-Luzerne); and other lawmakers will host a hearing titled \" Protecting the Fourth Amendment: Establishing Guardrails for Automated Surveillance\" to examine automated license plate reader technology and its implications for public safety, privacy, and the Fourth Amendment.\nWHO:\nTestifiers will include Lawrence Gerrity, legislative chairman, Pennsylvania Fraternal Order of Police; Jim Harper, nonresident senior fellow, American Enterprise Institute; Neal Kurk, former representative, New Hampshire House of Representatives.\nWHEN:\nMonday, Sept. 28, at 11 a.m.\nWHERE:\nRoom 418, House Republican Caucus Room,\nState Capitol, Harrisburg\n.\nLIVESTREAM:\nBarring any technical difficulties, the hearing will be streamed online at\npolicycommittee.com\n.\nMedia contact:\nNancy Nilson, 202-641-1233,\nnnilson@pahousegop.com\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "Pennsylvania House Republican legislators are hosting a policy committee hearing on automated license plate reader technology to examine its effects on privacy and the Fourth Amendment. The hearing will feature testimony from representatives of law enforcement, a think tank, and a former state legislator. It is scheduled for September 28, 2026 at the State Capitol in Harrisburg.",
      "action_type": "committee_action",
      "topics": [
        "public_safety",
        "other"
      ],
      "spin_flag": false
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Walsh Joins Colleagues in Fight Against Government Overreach",
    "url": "https://repwalsh.com/News/38813/Press-Releases/Walsh-Joins-Colleagues-in-Fight-Against-Government-Overreach",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "Walsh Joins Colleagues in Fight Against Government Overreach\nSeptember 25, 2026\nHARRISBURG\n– Earlier this week, state Rep. Jamie Walsh (R-Luzerne) joined 84 of his colleagues in submitting a letter calling for the withdrawal of the Pennsylvania Department of Health’s (DOH) proposed rewrite of regulations pertaining to communicable and noncommunicable diseases in the Commonwealth.\n“The proposal from the Department of Health would threaten the rights and privacy of every Pennsylvania resident,” said Walsh. “The people I represent don’t want the government superseding their parental rights when it comes to their children and they don’t want the government having access to their private medical records. These proposed regulations go too far.”\nThe letter, which was sent to the Independent Regulatory Review Commission (IRRC), highlights several provisions within the proposal, including:\n• Broadly expanding mandatory vaccine reporting and allowing increased access to private medical records.\n• Granting health officials the authority to enter school buildings and interview minor students privately for disease-control investigations without clear parental knowledge or consent.\n• Providing the ability to enter private residences for disease control investigations while remaining silent about whether a search warrant is required prior to entering a residence.\n• Expanding protocols for contact tracing, isolation and quarantine, while raising concerns over unilateral executive powers to re-impose mask mandates or other coercive measures.\n• Attempting to permanently establish sweeping public health powers through the regulatory process rather than through open legislative debate.\nUnder Pennsylvania law, IRRC is charged with reviewing proposed regulations to ensure they are in the public interest and do not exceed statutory authority.\n“I am hopeful the Independent Regulatory Review Commission, after reviewing our letter and the thousands of complaints they have received from Pennsylvania residents, disapproves the proposed changes,” said Walsh. “These types of sweeping changes should go through the legislative process, where they can be debated in an open forum. I question why this comprehensive overhaul was done without our input when we represent the very people these changes would impact.”\nClick here\nto read the comment letter.\nRepresentative Jamie Walsh\n117th Legislative District\nPennsylvania House of Representatives\nMedia Contact: Tricia Lehman\n717.772.9840\ntlehman@pahousegop.com\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "State Rep. Jamie Walsh co-signed a letter with 84 other legislators urging the Independent Regulatory Review Commission to reject proposed updates to Pennsylvania Department of Health regulations governing communicable and noncommunicable diseases. The letter objects to several provisions including expanded vaccine reporting, health officials interviewing minors without parental consent, potential warrantless entry into homes for disease investigations, and expanded quarantine and mask mandate authority. Walsh argues these changes should go through the legislature rather than the regulatory process.",
      "action_type": "statement",
      "topics": [
        "healthcare",
        "public_safety",
        "other"
      ],
      "spin_flag": true
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Walsh Seeks Service Photos for Nov. 7 Veterans Recognition Event",
    "url": "https://repwalsh.com/News/38793/Press-Releases/Walsh-Seeks-Service-Photos-for-Nov--7-Veterans-Recognition-Event",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "Walsh Seeks Service Photos for Nov. 7 Veterans Recognition Event\nSeptember 21, 2026\nHARRISBURG – State Rep. Jamie Walsh (R-Luzerne) will host a Veterans Recognition Event and Expo for Luzerne County veterans on Saturday, Nov. 7, in partnership with Misericordia University. As part of the event, he is asking for service photos of those planning to attend.\n“I am very much looking forward to this event,” said Walsh. “No one deserves our gratitude more than the men and women who have faithfully served this country. This event is one small way for us show our respect and support. Another way we would like to honor those in attendance is with display boards at the event showcasing photos of our veterans during their time in service. If you are attending and have a photo to share, please send an electronic copy or stop by my Dallas office to have it scanned.”\nThe event will include a recognition ceremony along with dozens of vendors offering programs and services for veterans and their family members. Light refreshments will be served, and members of the United States Army Field Band will perform during the ceremony.\nThe event will be held from 10 a.m. to 1 p.m. at Lemmond Theater and Insalaco Hall, Misericordia University, 301 Lake St. in Dallas.\nThose who would like to provide a service photo to be displayed at this year’s event are asked to email the photo to CSweeney@PAHouseGOP.com, or bring the photo to the Dallas district office, at 2825 Memorial Highway in Dallas, or Sugarloaf district office, at 669 State Route 93, Suite 3, for scanning.\nRegistration is required for this event; veterans can each bring one guest. Please RSVP by Oct. 24 by calling 570-675-6000 or going online to\nRepWalsh.com\n.\nRepresentative Jamie Walsh\n117th Legislative District\nPennsylvania House of Representatives\nMedia Contact: Tricia Lehman\n717-772-9840\ntlehman@pahousegop.com\nRepWalsh.com / Facebook.com/RepJamieWalsh\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "State Rep. Jamie Walsh is organizing a Veterans Recognition Event and Expo on November 7 at Misericordia University in Dallas, PA, open to Luzerne County veterans and one guest each. The event will include a recognition ceremony, vendor booths offering veteran services, refreshments, and a performance by the U.S. Army Field Band. Walsh is also collecting service-era photos from attending veterans to display at the event.",
      "action_type": "event",
      "topics": [
        "other"
      ],
      "spin_flag": false
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Walsh Releases Statement Regarding State’s “Rain Tax”",
    "url": "https://repwalsh.com/News/38782/Press-Releases/Walsh-Releases-Statement-Regarding-State-s--Rain-Tax",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "Walsh Releases Statement Regarding State’s “Rain Tax”\nSeptember 17, 2026\nHARRISBURG\n– State Rep. Jamie Walsh (R-Luzerne) today released the following statement regarding an executive order signed by President Donald Trump to reverse federal stormwater managements fees, known as the “Rain Tax”:\n“Yesterday, President Trump reversed an Obama-era executive order responsible for the creation of onerous stormwater management fees – also known as the Rain Tax – that have burdened families and businesses in northeastern Pennsylvania since its enactment. President Trump’s executive order eases the pain of a regulatory framework that has caused Luzerne County residents to pay exorbitant bills caused solely by the rain that falls on their houses and driveways. This executive order delivers long-awaited relief for our area, and I’m especially grateful to Congressmen Rob Bresnahan and Dan Meuser, who have led the fight to free northeastern Pennsylvania from the Rain Tax. This achievement is the result of their tireless efforts at the federal level.\n“This action imparts an obligation to the Pennsylvania Legislature to codify this relief into state law by passing\nHouse Bill 2360\n, legislation written by Rep. Brenda Pugh that I have signed onto as a co-sponsor. This bill would replace the Municipal Separate Storm Sewer System (MS4) permit requirements with a more equitable, grant-funded formula.\n“The Pennsylvania House of Representatives and Senate have very few session days remaining before the end of the term. I urge my colleagues in both the House and Senate to act on this opportunity to lift the burden of the Rain Tax from our homeowners and municipalities.”\nRepresentative Jamie Walsh\n117th Legislative District\nPennsylvania House of Representatives\nMedia Contact: Tricia Lehman\n717.772.9840\ntlehman@pahousegop.com\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "State Rep. Jamie Walsh issued a statement praising a federal executive order by President Trump that reverses stormwater management fee requirements, commonly called the 'Rain Tax.' Walsh called on the Pennsylvania Legislature to pass House Bill 2360, a state bill he co-sponsored that would replace current stormwater permit requirements with a grant-funded system. Walsh himself did not introduce legislation or take a direct legislative action; he released a statement and urged colleagues to act before the end of the legislative term.",
      "action_type": "statement",
      "topics": [
        "environment",
        "economy",
        "infrastructure"
      ],
      "spin_flag": true
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Walsh Announces $895,160 in Grant Funding for Projects in the 117th District",
    "url": "https://repwalsh.com/News/38689/Press-Releases/Walsh-Announces--895-160-in-Grant-Funding-for-Projects-in-the-117th-District",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "Walsh Announces $895,160 in Grant Funding for Projects in the 117th District\nSeptember 15, 2026\nHARRISBURG –\nRep. Jamie Walsh (R-Luzerne) today announced $895,160 in funding from the Local Share Account (LSA) grant program for public safety improvements, equipment purchases and infrastructure projects across the 117th District.\n“I was pleased to work with Sens. Dave Argall, Lisa Baker and Linda Schlegel Culver to advocate for funding of these projects, which will make a huge impact in their communities,” said Walsh. “This funding will help alleviate the financial burden on our municipalities without pulling from taxpayer dollars. I’m grateful such a large portion of funding has been allocated to our region.”\nMunicipalities receiving grants include:\n•\tBlack Creek Township - $114,522\nfor the purchase of a patching machine to repair roads in the township.\n•\tBlack Creek Township, on behalf of Nuremberg-Weston Volunteer Fire Company - $75,000\nfor the purchase of updated gear and equipment.\n•\tDallas Borough, on behalf of Misericordia University - $150,000\nfor pedestrian safety improvements at the roundabout in the borough.\n•\tHarvey’s Lake Borough - $254,308\nfor a hydrilla management project at Harvey’s Lake. The funding would specifically be used to destroy hydrilla, an invasive, non-native aquatic plant that is destroying the lake, as well as continued monitoring to determine the effectiveness of the treatment.\n•\tLuzerne County Redevelopment Authority, on behalf of Greater Wyoming Valley Area YMCA - $225,000\nfor construction of a new dining lodge at Camp Kresge in Dennison Township.\n•\tRoss Township - $76,330\nfor the purchase of a new mini excavator.\nThe grants were approved and awarded by the Commonwealth Financing Authority, which was established as an independent agency that holds fiduciary responsibility over the funding of programs and investments in Pennsylvania’s economic growth. LSA grants are derived from gaming revenues.\nRepresentative Jamie Walsh\n117th Legislative District\nPennsylvania House of Representatives\nMedia Contact: Tricia Lehman\n717.772.9840\ntlehman@pahousegop.com\nRepWalsh.com\n/\nFacebook.com/RepJamieWalsh\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "Rep. Jamie Walsh announced that six municipalities and organizations in Pennsylvania's 117th District received a combined $895,160 in Local Share Account grants, funded by gaming revenues and approved by the Commonwealth Financing Authority. The grants will fund road repair equipment, firefighter gear, pedestrian safety improvements, invasive plant removal at Harvey's Lake, a YMCA dining lodge, and a mini excavator. Walsh's role was to advocate for the funding alongside three state senators; he did not personally approve or award the grants.",
      "action_type": "other",
      "topics": [
        "infrastructure",
        "public_safety",
        "environment"
      ],
      "spin_flag": true
    }
