  {
    "title": "Walsh Supports Pennsylvania Ratepayer Protection Act to Lower Electricity Costs, Address Data Center Demand",
    "url": "https://repwalsh.com/News/37829/Press-Releases/Walsh-Supports-Pennsylvania-Ratepayer-Protection-Act-to-Lower-Electricity-Costs--Address-Data-Center-Demand",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "Walsh Supports Pennsylvania Ratepayer Protection Act to Lower Electricity Costs, Address Data Center Demand\nApril 13, 2026\nHARRISBURG – State Rep. Jamie Walsh (R-Luzerne) has announced he is a co-sponsor of legislation to protect consumers from rising electricity costs driven by the growing energy demands of large-scale data centers, while strengthening the state’s energy economy.\n“The proposed Pennsylvania Ratepayer Protection Act is a proactive, sensible piece of legislation to help address potential rising energy costs with the construction of new data centers,” said Walsh.\nHouse Bill 2372\nintroduces a three-part strategy to lower costs and stabilize the energy market:\n•\tRequires data center developers to fully fund their energy infrastructure and grid connections, ensuring no additional costs are passed on to consumers.\n•\tMandates large data centers build or secure their own power sources rather than relying on the public grid, reducing competition for limited electricity supply.\n•\tRequires utilities to enter long-term power purchase agreements on behalf of consumers, ensuring ratepayers have equal access to stable, lower-cost energy supplies.\nTogether, these requirements are expected to deliver immediate electricity price reductions; increase available power supply across the PJM region; protect consumers from subsidizing large-scale energy users; and support responsible growth of the data center industry.\n“By requiring data centers to meet their own energy needs and ensuring utilities secure long-term power for residents, the legislation aims to rebalance supply and demand, leading to lower prices and a more resilient energy market,” said Walsh.\nHouse Bill 2372 is awaiting assignment to a committee.\nRepresentative Jamie Walsh\n117th Legislative District\nPennsylvania House of Representatives\nMedia Contact: Tricia Lehman\n717.772.9840\ntlehman@pahousegop.com\nRepWalsh.com / Facebook.com/RepJamieWalsh\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "State Rep. Jamie Walsh signed on as a co-sponsor of House Bill 2372, which would require data centers to pay for their own grid connections and power sources rather than drawing from the shared public electricity grid. The bill would also require utilities to negotiate long-term power purchase agreements for consumers. The bill has been introduced but has not yet been assigned to a committee.",
      "action_type": "bill_introduced",
      "topics": [
        "economy",
        "infrastructure",
        "other"
      ],
      "spin_flag": true
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Walsh: Don’t Miss Out on These Upcoming Events",
    "url": "https://repwalsh.com/News/37813/Press-Releases/Walsh--Don-t-Miss-Out-on-These-Upcoming-Events",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "Walsh: Don’t Miss Out on These Upcoming Events\nApril 8, 2026\nHARRISBURG – State Rep. Jamie Walsh (R-Luzerne) is hosting several upcoming events for residents throughout the 117th Legislative District.\n“I have various events coming up that will assist residents with licensing their dogs, recycling old documents or electronics, avoiding ‘romance’ scams, and learning more about their Second Amendment rights in Pennsylvania,” said Walsh. “I enjoy providing these events and having the opportunity to talk with attendees about the state issues most important to them.”\nUpcoming events include:\n•\nPA Hunter/Trapper Education Class\n- Saturday, April 11, from 8 a.m. to 3 p.m. at the PA Game Commission Northeast Regional Office, 3917 Memorial Hwy. in Dallas. Lunch and drinks will be provided. This is a required class for all first-time hunters and trappers age 11 and older. Please RSVP at\nRepWalsh.com/events\nor call the Sugarloaf district office at 570-359-2138.\n•\nDog Licensing Event\n- Saturday, April 11, from 9 a.m. to noon at Walsh’s Sugarloaf district office at 669 State Route 93, Suite 3. Dog owners may choose to purchase single-year licenses or a lifetime license. To purchase a lifetime license, the dog must have some form of permanent identification, such as a microchip or tattoo. The owner will need a lifetime license application and the permanent identification verification (PIV) form to receive a lifetime license. Single-year licenses cost $10.80, while lifetime licenses cost $52.80. Senior citizens or people with disabilities may purchase a single-year license for $8.80 or a lifetime license for $36.80. Only cash, check or money order will be accepted for payment; NO credit cards.\n•\nRomance Scam Prevention\n- Friday, April 17, at noon at Meadows Manor Community Room, 200 Lake St. in Dallas. Lunch will be provided starting at 11:30 a.m. This event will be co-hosted with Rep. Brenda Pugh (R-Luzerne), and will help educate individuals on scams that use the illusion of a romantic or close relationship to manipulate and/or steal from the victim. The presenter will be a representative from the Pennsylvania Office of the Attorney General. Please RSVP at\nRepWalsh.com/events\nor call the Dallas district office at 570-675-6000.\n•\nShredding and E-Recycling Event\n- Saturday, April 18, from 11 a.m. to 2 p.m. at Butler Township Community Park, North Old Turnpike Road in Drums. This is an opportunity to dispose of paper documents containing personal, financial and medical information, as well as hard-to-recycle electronics. More information on what recycled items will be accepted is available on Walsh’s website at\nRepWalsh.com/events\n.\n•\nConcealed Carry Seminar\n- Wednesday, April 22, from 6-8 p.m. at the White Haven Area Community Library, 121 Towanda St. in White Haven. Luzerne County District Attorney Sam Sanguedolce will discuss the latest information about Pennsylvania’s firearms laws, including the state’s Castle Doctrine, and the process our gun owners need to follow to obtain a permit. Seating is limited. Please RSVP at\nRepWalsh.com/events\nor call the Sugarloaf district office at 570-359-2138.\nFor more information on these and other upcoming events, please visit Walsh’s website at\nRepWalsh.com\nand go to his “Events” page.\nRepresentative Jamie Walsh\n117th Legislative District\nPennsylvania House of Representatives\nMedia Contact: Tricia Lehman\n717.772.9840\ntlehman@pahousegop.com\nRepWalsh.com / Facebook.com/RepJamieWalsh\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "State Rep. Jamie Walsh is announcing five upcoming community events in his district scheduled for April 2026, including a hunter education class, dog licensing, romance scam prevention, document shredding and electronics recycling, and a concealed carry firearms seminar. These are constituent service events hosted at various locations throughout the 117th Legislative District. No legislation was introduced or voted on.",
      "action_type": "event",
      "topics": [
        "public_safety",
        "other"
      ],
      "spin_flag": false
    }
  },
