    "title": "Walsh: Funding for Area Fire, EMS Companies Announced",
    "url": "https://repwalsh.com/News/37545/Press-Releases/Walsh--Funding-for-Area-Fire--EMS-Companies-Announced",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "Walsh: Funding for Area Fire, EMS Companies Announced\nFebruary 13, 2026\nHARRISBURG\n– Rep. Jamie Walsh (R-Luzerne) announced funding is being awarded to 34 emergency responder organizations in the 117th Legislative District under the Fire Company and Emergency Medical Services Grant Program.\n“Our fire and EMS companies are the backbone of public safety in our communities,” said Walsh. “These grants recognize their unwavering dedication and provide critical resources to help them continue saving lives and protecting property. I am proud to support these brave men and women who answer the call every day, and I thank them for their service.”\nThe following is a list of local fire and EMS companies and the amount of their grant awards:\n•\nBack Mountain Regional Fire and EMS (EMS) - $30,000.\n•\nBack Mountain Regional Fire and EMS (Fire) - $26,693.\n•\nDennison Township Volunteer Fire Company No. 1 - $12,220.\n•\nDorrance Township Volunteer Fire Department (EMS) - $15,000.\n•\nDorrance Township Volunteer Fire Department (Fire) - $12,426.\n•\nFairmount Township Volunteer Fire and Ambulance Co. (EMS) - $15,000.\n•\nFairmount Township Volunteer Fire and Ambulance Co. (Fire) - $12,220.\n•\nFearnots Volunteer Fire Co. - $12,838.\n•\nFreeland Fire Department - $14,899.\n•\nFreeland Northside Community Ambulance Association - $15,000.\n•\nHarveys Lake Fire and Ambulance Association (EMS) - $15,000.\n•\nHarveys Lake Fire and Ambulance Association (Fire) - $12,014.\n•\nHobbie Volunteer Fire Company No. 1 - $12,426.\n•\nHunlock Creek Volunteer Fire Co. - $12,014.\n•\nJonathan R. Davis Volunteer Fire Company DBA Idetown Fire Company - $12,220.\n•\nLake Silkworth Volunteer Fire Company - $12,632.\n•\nMocanaqua Volunteer Fire Co. No. 1 Inc. - $12,426.\n•\nNescopeck Township Volunteer Fire Company No. 1 - $12,838.\n•\nNescopeck Volunteer Fire Co. No. 1 - $12,014.\n•\nNuremberg Weston Volunteer Fire Company - $13,663.\n•\nPond Hill-Lily Lake Ambulance Association - $15,000.\n•\nPond Hill-Lily Lake Volunteer Fire Company - $12,014.\n•\nSalem Township Volunteer Fire Company - $25,457.\n•\nShickshinny Volunteer Ambulance Association Inc. - $15,000.\n•\nSlocum Township Volunteer Ambulance - $15,000.\n•\nSlocum Township Volunteer Fire Co. No. 1 - $12,632.\n•\nSugarloaf Fire Company (EMS) - $15,000.\n•\nSugarloaf Fire Company (Fire) - $13,044.\n•\nSweet Valley Ambulance Association Inc. - $15,000.\n•\nSweet Valley Volunteer Fire Co. - $14,487\n•\nValley Regional Fire and Rescue Inc. (EMS) - $15,000.\n•\nValley Regional Fire and Rescue Inc. (Fire) - $13,663.\n•\nWhite Haven Fire Company No. 1 - $13,457.\n•\nWhite Haven Rescue Unit Inc. - $15,000.\nProjects eligible for funding under the grant program include construction or renovation of a fire or ambulance company facility, purchase or repair of equipment, training, reduction of existing debt, recruitment/retention, and public education about fire safety.\nThe funding comes from an ongoing grant program created by the Legislature and administered by the Pennsylvania Emergency Management Agency and Office of the State Fire Commissioner (OSFC). All funding comes from the proceeds from slot machine gaming, and not General Fund tax revenue.\nRepresentative Jamie Walsh\n117th Legislative District\nPennsylvania House of Representatives\nMedia Contact: Tricia Lehman\n717.772.9840\ntlehman@pahousegop.com\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "Rep. Jamie Walsh announced that 34 fire and EMS organizations in his district will receive grants totaling approximately $487,000 through Pennsylvania's Fire Company and Emergency Medical Services Grant Program. The grants range from about $12,000 to $30,000 per award and can be used for equipment, facility construction or renovation, training, debt reduction, and recruitment. The funding comes from a pre-existing state program funded by slot machine gaming revenue, not a new initiative Walsh created.",
      "action_type": "statement",
      "topics": [
        "public_safety"
      ],
      "spin_flag": true
    }
